  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale:\n   We aim to find an improved upper bound for the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem constant C by optimizing a step function on [0,2]. Unlike the parent's algorithm which uses mixed continuous and discrete optimizations, we adopt a hierarchical Bayesian optimization approach that explicitly models the structure of the problem. The core idea is to recast the optimization problem into a multi-resolution framework where we simultaneously explore coarse-grained structures that minimize the potential maximum overlap.\n\n   Our method will use Gaussian processes to model the objective function and its gradients over multiple resolutions. By maintaining a probability distribution over promising candidate solutions, we escape local optima more reliably than local search methods. This approach leverages the problem's structure (periodic nature of optimal solutions, energy localization phenomena) rather than treating it as a pure black-box optimization.\n\n   Key limitations include potential parameter sensitivity and high dimensionality, but we address this by progressive resolution adjustment and gradient-based covariance estimation. The rationale is that Bayesian optimization with multi-resolution embedding can better capture the combinatorial structure at the heart of this challenging non-convex problem.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification:\n   Let h:[0,2] \u2192 [0,1] be a step function with integral 1. We discretize the domain [0,2] to M = 5x192 points (ensuring sufficient resolution for function features). Define:\n   C(h) = max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\n\n   We model the correlation between shifts using an energy function:\n   E(h) = \u2211_{k=-M+1}^{M-1} e^{-\u03b2C_k(h)}\n\n   where \u03b2 is a temperature parameter. The optimization problem becomes:\n   minimize_h E(h) subject to 1/2 \u2264 \u222bh(x)dx \u2264 1 (binary option) or continuous bounds\n\n   Our hierarchical approach introduces a multi-scale covariance kernel:\n   k(x_i,x_j) = \u03c3^2 exp(-|log x_i - log x_j|/l) + \u03b4(x_i-x_j)\u03c3_perio^2\n\n   The \u03b4 kernel models periodic behavior, and the log-difference term captures scale similarity.\n\n3. Implementation Procedure:\n   Phase 1: Initialize multi-resolution hierarchy\n     - Start with parent's provided grid (960 samples) at level 5\n     - Compute coarse levels: levels 1-4 by down-sampling (factor 2 each time)\n     - Level 0: Random 16-point binary initialization\n     - Use Bayesian optimization to select promising coarse-level solutions\n\n   Phase 2: Hierarchical Bayesian optimization\n     - At each coarse level L:\n       1. Compute objective using all shifts for grid points\n       2. Maintain Gaussian Process model with adaptive covariance\n       3. Acquire next point using expected improvement criterion\n       4. Evaluate candidate solution with gradient estimation\n       5. Determine which isoclines (function level sets) to explore next\n\n   Phase 3: Progressive Resolution Refinement\n     - When a coarse-level solution shows improvement:\n       1. Convert candidate to next resolution: Linear interpolation\n       2. Add compactly supported features at four locations\n       3. Repair feasibility by local overhaul\n       4. Refine using gradient projection method\n\n   Phase 4: Global Checkpoint\n     - Every 100 evaluations:\n       1. Compare running best against initial\n       2. Switch success probability threshold if:\n          - Improvement > 5% for 3 consecutive reps\n          - Flat region detected in GP expectations\n\n   Phase 5: Early Termination & Recovery\n     - If evaluation rate falls below 2.5 per second:\n       1. Gradually reduce resolution to L from current level\n       2. Switch to active set method for function evaluation\n     - If budget nears 1200 seconds:\n       1. Estimate time-to-optimum based GP model\n       2. Scale remaining resources to focus on best try\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations:\n   Default configuration:\n     - Coarsest level (L=0): 16 points, binary initial\n     - Level scaling: geometric factor 2 each level\n     - GP model: Mat\u00e9rn1/2 covariance with adaptive lengthscale\n     - Acquisition: Expected improvement with probability scaling\n     - Refinement factor: 4 new features per level\n\n   Variations:\n   A. Progressive Cooling (Mode1):\n      - Adaptive \u03b2 = 1.5 during exploration, 0.8 during refinement\n      - Cooldown threshold: entropy-based distribution narrowing\n\n   B. Dynamic Resolution (Mode2):\n      - Add resolution if max correlation < 0.38 or plateauing\n\n   C. Energy-Directed Sampling (Mode3):\n      - Where to add overlap-critical points: measure by gradient\n      - Use in when entropy estimates sparse regions\n\n   D. Training Set Expansion (Mode4):\n      - Use 20 sets of previous evaluations as training points\n      - Triggered by poor improvement rate\n\n   E. Fresh Initialization (Mode5):\n      - Regenerate stepped function if model history reversal\n\n   Transition rules:\n     - Switch between modes based on grid density and plateauing history\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness:\n   Time allocation:\n     - GP training: ~20% budget\n     - Objective eval: ~60%\n     - Refinement: ~15%\n     - Hierarchical transitions: ~5%\n\n   Feasibility checks:\n     - Maintain array length constant: 480 for binary, 960 for continuous\n     - Repair function: use constrained Steepest Descent\n     - Time budget: monitor every 3 seconds\n\n   Resource constraints:\n     - Memory: 1.2GB allocated (plus runtime's 1GB)\n     - Multi-process: not available, use single threaded\n     - Fallback: 4-parameter adaptive step function\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract:\n   Final candidate validation:\n     - Re-Eval: Warmup random check + actual objective\n     - Format: (h_values, c5_bound, n_points)\n     - c5_bound: Actual computed max overlap with parent's dx\n     - n_points: 480 or 960\n\n   Documentation:\n     - Return grid representation (0.5 is binary threshold)\n     - Log sheets every 15 improvements\n     - Clean termination on budget or success contraction\n\n   Error handling:\n     - NaN values: replace with best known\n     - Infeasible candidates: repair by projection\n     - Stalled progress: swap coarse/fine adaptations\n\n   Statistical tracking:\n     - Success probability: rolling 100 improvement window\n     - Objective history: save every milestone\n     - Memory accounting: ~0.8 for floating point",
